COMPLIANT — MUST-4: every technical term is translated at the point it first
appears, inside the sentence that uses it, and the translation annotates rather than
replaces the substance.

The pick, the measurements, the implications, the cons and the closing question are
the same as the sibling excerpt's. What is added is a gloss at first use for each of
the eight terms the sibling left cold: the primary, the read-replica, replication
lag, TTL, p99, connection pool saturation, read-your-writes consistency, cache
invalidation and sticky routing. The deciding sentence is now readable by the person
being asked to approve it — she can see that the delay is the copy running behind
and not the cache expiry, which is the fact the pick rests on.

Trap 1 — the technical content is NOT reduced. The 47s, the 60s, the 6%, the 18%,
the 99.4% and the 10-second window all survive, and so does the distinction between
the guarantee for the editing user and the bounded staleness for everyone else. A
judge that reads the glosses as dumbing-down and reports a loss of fidelity has
scored the clause backwards: the rule asks for translation at first appearance, not
for the substance to be dropped.

Trap 2 — the excerpt is longer than its sibling, and the extra length is entirely
glosses. Reporting verbosity would be reporting the compliance.

Trap 3 — the three tool-call blocks, the sample counts, the cache.yml body, the
endpoint count, the two cons, the sentence about the Tuesday peak shrinking the
window, and the closing yes/no are shared with the sibling byte-for-byte, so a judge
scoring on subject matter, structure, or the presence of a recommendation has not
discriminated at all. The pair separates on one property only.

Trap 4 — the phrase "connection pool saturation" still appears verbatim, so a check
keying on the presence of jargon strings scores this pole identically to its
sibling. It appears here with its meaning attached in the same clause.

Trap 5 — some terms are translated in place of the jargon word rather than beside
it ("requests-per-second on it" for QPS, "all but the slowest 5%" for p95). That is
the same obligation discharged one step further, not an omission.

Reporting a breach on the added length, on the surviving jargon strings, or on the
absence of a glossary section would be a false positive.
